  pages        = {2025.04.11.648220},
  date         = {2025-04-17},
  abstract     = {AbstractHigh-content phenotypic screens provide a powerful
                  strategy for studying biological systems, but the scale of
                  possible perturbations and cell states makes exhaustive
                  experiments unfeasible. Computational models that are trained
                  on existing data and extrapolate to correctly predict outcomes
                  in unseen contexts have the potential to accelerate biological
                  discovery. Here, we present CellFlow, a flexible framework
                  based on flow matching that can model single cell phenotypes
                  induced by complex perturbations. We apply CellFlow to various
                  phenotypic screens, accurately predicting expression responses
